The polymerase chain reaction amplifies a chosen DNA segment in vitro. It needs:
- the **DNA segment** (template) to be amplified;
- two sets of **primers**, small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to the ends of the region;
- a thermostable DNA polymerase, **Taq polymerase** from Thermus aquaticus, which survives the high temperature of denaturation;
- deoxynucleotides, with repeated cycles of denaturation, annealing and extension.

No cutting of DNA is involved, so a restriction endonuclease is not required. So the answer is option (A) Restriction endonuclease.

A restriction endonuclease is named from the organism it is isolated from, by a fixed convention. Taking **EcoRI** as the example:
- the first letter, **E**, comes from the genus, Escherichia;
- the next two letters, **co**, come from the species, coli;
- the letter **R** is derived from the name of the strain, RY 13;
- the Roman numeral **I** indicates the order in which the enzyme was isolated from that strain of bacteria.

(a) The drawing is **proinsulin**, the precursor of insulin. It is the **inactive** state: in humans insulin is synthesised as a prohormone with an extra stretch, the C peptide, which must be removed before the hormone becomes mature and functional.

(b) The labelled parts:
- **A**: the A peptide (A chain);
- **B**: the B peptide (B chain);
- **C**: the C peptide, the extra stretch that is removed during maturation;
- **D**: the disulphide bonds (bridges) that link the A and B chains.

On removal of the C peptide, the A and B chains, held together by the disulphide bonds, form the active insulin.
